# Tracked chassis exercising the 'track' generator block. A floating hull carries
# a drive sprocket, an idler (the two 'end' pulleys), and two road wheels; the
# track block expands into a closed band of 40 shoes riding on them. Geometry is
# placeholder; the point is the expansion, not real HMMWV data.
#
# The band is CENTRED ON THE HULL (y = 0) rather than offset to one side. A real
# vehicle has two of these straddling the centreline, and their loads balance in
# roll; one band alone, offset 0.5 m from a centreline CG, is a monocycle stood
# on its edge -- it never reaches a static attitude and the settled ground load
# ran 11% over the vehicle weight while it kept toppling. Centring it makes the
# single band statically sound, which is what this model needs to be. m113 is
# where two real bands are exercised. See track_min.mech for the smallest model
# that exercises road-wheel face contact at all.

gravity 0 0 -9.81
stab 0.05

body                     # 0: hull, free-flying
parent -1
joint floating
tree 0 0 0
mass 4000
cg 0 0 0.4
inertia 2000 6000 6500 0 0 0
fext 1

body                     # 1: drive sprocket (rear end pulley)
parent 0
joint revolute y
tree -1.0 0 0.30
mass 80
cg 0 0 0
inertia 4 7 4 0 0 0
fext 1                   # contact reaction from the track band

body                     # 2: idler (front end pulley)
parent 0
joint revolute y
tree 1.0 0 0.30
mass 60
cg 0 0 0
inertia 3 5 3 0 0 0
fext 1                   # contact reaction from the track band

body                     # 3: road wheel 1
parent 0
joint revolute y
tree -0.35 0 0.20      # z chosen so the wheel is TANGENT to the belt's lower
                         # run: the end pulleys sit at z 0.30 with radius 0.25,
                         # so the run is at z 0.05, and 0.20 + 0.15 puts this
                         # wheel exactly on it. Unlike m113 this model has no
                         # road-wheel poke-through, which is the point -- it
                         # exercises ground contact without the belt
                         # idealization confounding the result
mass 50
cg 0 0 0
inertia 2 3 2 0 0 0
fext 1

body                     # 4: road wheel 2
parent 0
joint revolute y
tree 0.35 0 0.20       # tangent to the lower run, as above
mass 50
cg 0 0 0
inertia 2 3 2 0 0 0
fext 1

track demo_band
root 0
plane xz
shoes 40                            # not 12: an END roller is carried by the pins
                                    # either side of it, so the shoe pitch has
                                    # to stay under the sprocket/idler DIAMETER
                                    # or those pulleys fall straight between two
                                    # pins. 12 shoes gave a 0.464 m pitch against
                                    # 0.50 m end pulleys; 40 gives 0.139 m.
                                    # This says nothing about the ROAD wheels --
                                    # they roll on the shoe's flat inner face,
                                    # which cannot be fallen between at any
                                    # pitch. The original note here reasoned
                                    # from the road wheels and was wrong about
                                    # why the number had to change.
pitch 0.139
mass 5.0
cg 0 0 0
inertia 0.01 0.03 0.03 0 0 0
fext 1
roller 1 0.25 end drive teeth 11   # sprocket: 2*pi*0.25/11 = 0.1428 m tooth
                                   # pitch, within 2.5% of the 0.1393 shoe pitch
roller 2 0.25 end                  # idler
roller 3 0.15                       # road wheel 1
roller 4 0.15                       # road wheel 2
pad -0.05 0.11 0.30                 # ground pad: 0.05 below the pin line, inset
                                    # from the 0.139 link so adjacent pads do
                                    # not put corners on the shared pins
thickness 0.05                      # running face on the pin plane; the road
                                    # wheels roll on it, the pins engage only
                                    # the sprocket and idler
kn 3.5e6
kt 1.0e6
mu 0.8

# Ground. The lower run is tangent to all four rollers at z = 0.05 and the pad
# face is 0.05 below that, so the design pose IS the ride height and the model
# starts at zero penetration -- it sinks by its static deflection and no more.
ground
plane 0 0 1 0.0
kn 2.0e5
mu 0.8
